Designing a New vLLM Auto-Tuner to incorporate the following capabilities:

- Intelligent Profiling: Automatically monitor GPU memory usage, compute utilization (via NVML or CUDA APIs) and vLLM metrics (e.g. prefill vs. decode times, preemption counts) to estimate headroom and bottlenecks.

- Adaptive Parameter Search: Use automated search algorithms like Bayesian optimization to adjust parameters such as batch_size, max_num_batched_tokens, max_num_seqs, and gpu_memory_utilization until throughput is maximized without OOMs. Support multi-objective optimization to trade off latency vs. throughput as needed.

- vLLM-Aware Integration: Leverage vLLM server logs and telemetry (KV cache utilization, slot occupancy, etc.) to guide tuning decisions. For instance, detect frequent cache preemptions (indicating memory shortage) and automatically raise gpu_memory_utilization or lower max_num_seqs.

- Multi-GPU and Parallelism Support: Handle both data-parallel and model-parallel (tensor/pipeline) configurations. The tuner should account for overhead of tensor/pipeline parallelism and tune parallel sizes alongside batch parameters to fit very large models.

- User-Friendly Configuration: Provide simple configuration (e.g. YAML) to specify tuning objectives (throughput target, latency bound, memory budget). Offer CLI interface for running tuning studies, and export the best configurations in a reusable format.

- Rich Metrics and Reporting: Produce detailed reports or dashboards (e.g. live progress or post-run summary) showing how different parameter settings affected throughput and latency. Include GPU telemetry charts to help users interpret the results.

- Extensibility: Allow custom benchmark workloads (via GuideLLM or user prompts) and plugins (e.g. for different quantization schemes) so the tuner can adapt to specific deployment scenarios.
